            }

            .report-header h1 {
                font-size: 1.8em;
            }

            .section {
                padding: 30px 20px;
            }
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <div class="report-container">
        <!-- Header -->
        <header class="report-header">
            <h1>Nine Decades of Oscar-Winning Scores</h1>
            <p class="subtitle">A Historical Trend Analysis (1934–2026)</p>
            <p class="meta">93 Ceremonies • 79 Unique Composers • 93 Award-Winning Scores</p>
        </header>

        <!-- Executive Summary / KPI Section -->
        <section class="section">
            <h2>Executive Summary</h2>

            <div class="kpi-grid">
                <div class="kpi-card">
                    <span class="value">79</span>
                    <span class="label">Total Unique Composers</span>
                </div>
                <div class="kpi-card">
                    <span class="value">4</span>
                    <span class="label">Maximum Wins by One Composer</span>
                </div>
                <div class="kpi-card">
                    <span class="value">29%</span>
                    <span class="label">Repeat Winner Rate</span>
                </div>
                <div class="kpi-card">
                    <span class="value">1940s</span>
                    <span class="label">Most Diverse Decade (12 unique)</span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="highlight-box">
                <p><strong>Three composers share the record for most wins (4):</strong> John Barry, John Williams, and Ludwig Göransson—spanning different eras from the Silver Age to Contemporary cinema. This rare tie reflects both the difficulty of repeat wins and the distinct dominance periods each composer commanded.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- Top Composers Section -->
        <section class="section">
            <h2>Most Awarded Composers</h2>

            <div class="chart-grid">
                <div class="chart-container">
                    <div class="chart-title">Top 10 Composers by Win Count</div>
                    <canvas id="topComposersChart"></canvas>
                </div>
                <div class="chart-container">
                    <div class="chart-title">Wins Distribution</div>
                    <canvas id="winsDistributionChart"></canvas>
                </div>
            </div>

            <table>
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th class="rank">#</th>
                        <th>Composer</th>
                        <th>Wins</th>
                        <th>Years</th>
                        <th>Notable Films</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td class="rank">1</td>
                        <td><strong>John Barry</strong></td>
                        <td class="wins">4</td>
                        <td>1966, 1968, 1985, 1990</td>
                        <td><em>Born Free, The Lion in Winter, Out of Africa, Dances with Wolves</em></td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td class="rank">1</td>
                        <td><strong>John Williams</strong></td>
                        <td class="wins">4</td>
                        <td>1975, 1977, 1982, 1993</td>
                        <td><em>Jaws, Star Wars, E.T., Schindler's List</em></td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td class="rank">1</td>
                        <td><strong>Ludwig Göransson</strong></td>
                        <td class="wins">4</td>
                        <td>2018, 2023, 2024, 2026</td>
                        <td><em>Black Panther, Oppenheimer (×2), Sinners</em></td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td class="rank">4</td>
                        <td>Max Steiner</td>
                        <td class="wins">3</td>
                        <td>1935, 1943, 1945</td>
                        <td><em>The Informer, Now, Voyager, Since You Went Away</em></td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td class="rank">4</td>
                        <td>Dimitri Tiomkin</td>
                        <td class="wins">3</td>
                        <td>1952, 1954, 1958</td>
                        <td><em>High Noon, The High and the Mighty, The Old Man and the Sea</em></td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td class="rank">4</td>
                        <td>Maurice Jarre</td>
                        <td class="wins">3</td>
                        <td>1962, 1965, 1984</td>
                        <td><em>Lawrence of Arabia, Doctor Zhivago, A Passage to India</em></td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td class="rank">4</td>
                        <td>Alan Menken</td>
                        <td class="wins">3</td>
                        <td>1989, 1991, 1992</td>
                        <td><em>The Little Mermaid, Beauty and the Beast, Aladdin</em></td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>

            <p>The three-way tie for first place masks a fascinating temporal dimension: these composers never competed directly. Barry dominated the 1960s–1990s, Williams the 1970s–1990s, and Göransson emerged in the 2010s–2020s. Each achieved their record in distinct eras, suggesting that the Academy's taste cycles through dominant voices rather than a continuous meritocratic competition.</p>
        </section>

        <!-- Decade Analysis Section -->
        <section class="section">
            <h2>Temporal Patterns & Decade-by-Decade Breakdown</h2>

            <div class="chart-grid">
                <div class="chart-container chart-full">
                    <div class="chart-title">Decade-by-Decade Winner Distribution</div>
                    <canvas id="decadeChart"></canvas>
                </div>
            </div>

            <table>
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th>Decade</th>
                        <th>Total Wins</th>
                        <th>Unique Composers</th>
                        <th>Top Composer</th>
                        <th>Key Pattern</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>1930s</strong></td>
                        <td>6</td>
                        <td>9</td>
                        <td>Louis Silvers, Max Steiner (1 each)</td>
                        <td>Highest diversity; founding era of the category</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>1940s</strong></td>
                        <td>10</td>
                        <td>12</td>
                        <td>Max Steiner (2 wins)</td>
                        <td>Most diverse decade; Golden Age in full bloom</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>1950s</strong></td>
                        <td>10</td>
                        <td>7</td>
                        <td>Dimitri Tiomkin (3 wins)</td>
                        <td>Most concentrated; Tiomkin dominates</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>1960s</strong></td>
                        <td>10</td>
                        <td>9</td>
                        <td>Maurice Jarre (2 wins)</td>
                        <td>Transition era; British composers emerge</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>1970s</strong></td>
                        <td>10</td>
                        <td>9</td>
                        <td>Nino Rota (2 wins)</td>
                        <td>New Hollywood; Williams enters strongly</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>1980s</strong></td>
                        <td>10</td>
                        <td>12</td>
                        <td>John Barry, Alan Menken (1 each)</td>
                        <td>Equal distribution; blockbuster era begins</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>1990s</strong></td>
                        <td>10</td>
                        <td>9</td>
                        <td>Alan Menken (3 wins)</td>
                        <td>Disney animation dominance</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>2000s</strong></td>
                        <td>10</td>
                        <td>8</td>
                        <td>Howard Shore (2 wins)</td>
                        <td>Fantasy epic cycle; Lord of the Rings</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>2010s</strong></td>
                        <td>10</td>
                        <td>10</td>
                        <td>Alexandre Desplat (2 wins)</td>
                        <td>Most balanced; diverse winners</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>2020s</strong></td>
                        <td>7</td>
                        <td>7</td>
                        <td>Ludwig Göransson (3 wins)</td>
                        <td>Göransson dominance; historic streak</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>

            <div class="highlight-box">
                <p><strong>The 1940s saw the highest composer diversity</strong> with 12 unique winners across 10 ceremonies—reflecting an era when many distinguished composers competed. By contrast, the <strong>1950s saw the most concentrated dominance</strong> with Dimitri Tiomkin winning 3 of 10 awards. The 2020s show a return to concentrated dominance with Göransson claiming 3 of 7 awards so far.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- Era Analysis Section -->
        <section class="section">
            <h2>Era-Based Analysis</h2>

            <div class="era-grid">
                <div class="era-card">
                    <div class="era-header">Golden Age (1934–1959)</div>
                    <div class="era-body">
                        <p>The founding era establishing orchestral film music conventions. Max Steiner pioneered the approach of scoring to narrative action, creating the template for cinematic composition.</p>
                        <div class="era-stats">
                            <div class="era-stat">
                                <div class="num">26</div>
                                <div class="desc">Wins</div>
                            </div>
                            <div class="era-stat">
                                <div class="num">25</div>
                                <div class="desc">Unique Composers</div>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <p style="margin-top:15px"><strong>Dominant Voices:</strong> Max Steiner (3), Dimitri Tiomkin (3), Alfred Newman (2)</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="era-card">
                    <div class="era-header">Silver Age (1960–1979)</div>
                    <div class="era-body">
                        <p>Modern Hollywood's emergence brought orchestral grandeur to epic scales. John Barry and Maurice Jarre defined the heroic, sweeping style that became synonymous with prestige filmmaking.</p>
                        <div class="era-stats">
                            <div class="era-stat">
                                <div class="num">20</div>
                                <div class="desc">Wins</div>
                            </div>
                            <div class="era-stat">
                                <div class="num">18</div>
                                <div class="desc">Unique Composers</div>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <p style="margin-top:15px"><strong>Dominant Voices:</strong> Maurice Jarre (2), John Barry (2), Nino Rota (2)</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="era-card">
                    <div class="era-header">Modern Era (1980–1999)</div>
                    <div class="era-body">
                        <p>The blockbuster era transformed Academy tastes toward fantasy and animated scores. John Williams' Star Wars legacy loomed large, while Alan Menken's Disney run dominated the late 1980s–1990s.</p>
                        <div class="era-stats">
                            <div class="era-stat">
                                <div class="num">20</div>
                                <div class="desc">Wins</div>
                            </div>
                            <div class="era-stat">
                                <div class="num">18</div>
                                <div class="desc">Unique Composers</div>
